LPA KIV-2: about 12 KIV-2 repeats in total over both copies (95% interval for read sampling, 12.0 to 12.0; aligner or library bias not included): fewer KIV-2 repeats than most people (the whole 95% interval is under 30; about 70% of the 1000 Genomes samples had 30 to 45): a low count goes with small apo(a) isoforms and a higher Lp(a) level (KIV-2 exons 30.00x, single-copy exons 30.00x, MAPQ 0 share 0.900)
LPA LPA KIV-2 4925G>A (a splice variant that lowers the Lp(a) made from its chromosome, Coassin 2017): not seen, 0 of 280 reads at its seven GRCh38 copies carry it. Not seen in the reads is not proof of absence: one carrying repeat among many gives few reads, and a short-read fraction never says which chromosome or repeat holds a variant.
